WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.379 --> 00:00:01.379
(bubbles popping)

00:00:01.379 --> 00:00:02.520
(screen whooshing)

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:04.739
- I (beeping) hate American breakfast.

00:00:04.739 --> 00:00:06.400
- [Child] Right away, I
need a good breakfast.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.610
- [Man] Right away, a big bite of toast.

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.020
- [Man] For kids, we like a
square meal for breakfast.

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:12.750
- [Man] A wonderful new
fruit in the box cereal

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:14.931
(bell ringing)

00:00:14.931 --> 00:00:16.630
(upbeat music)

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:18.830
- I mean, how did we get here?

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:20.110
How did this
(fluid moving)

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:22.690
become breakfast in my country?

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:24.700
And what is breakfast even all about?

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:26.830
Some people say it's the most
important meal of the day,

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:29.060
and others say that
it's totally worthless,

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:30.130
and you don't even need to eat breakfast

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:31.060
if you don't want to.

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:33.820
- [Man] Two pancakes with
hot syrup and butter.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:35.687
- I bet a lot of you
watching don't eat breakfast

00:00:35.687 --> 00:00:36.850
in the morning,

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:39.130
and you knew who else doesn't
eat breakfast in the morning?

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:39.963
This guy.

00:00:39.963 --> 00:00:40.943
- [Interviewer] what do
you eat for breakfast?

00:00:40.943 --> 00:00:41.776
- Nothing.

00:00:41.776 --> 00:00:45.039
- I grew up thinking that
breakfast had to be a certain way,

00:00:45.039 --> 00:00:46.360
(funky music)

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:47.770
but then in my twenties,

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.340
I started traveling and
I learned that in fact,

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.670
this version of breakfast
is a very narrow,

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:56.083
very American version of breakfast.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:00.640
Once I discovered
(pan sizzling)

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:02.510
non-American breakfast,
(liquid pouring)

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:04.310
I feel like my eyes were opened

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:06.980
to a completely different approach

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:08.530
to the first meal of the day.

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:10.410
I want to show you some
of those breakfasts,

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:12.760
but first, let's talk about breakfast

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.540
and how American breakfast got this way.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:16.840
- [Man] for about 22 cents,

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.741
You get as much nutrition as
this bacon and egg breakfast

00:01:19.741 --> 00:01:22.000
when there's no time for
your regular breakfast.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.860
- Okay, before I jump
into all of my opinions

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:25.110
about breakfast,

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:28.139
let's talk about science
for a minute, like facts.

00:01:28.139 --> 00:01:29.540
(mouse clicking)

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:30.820
does breakfast matter?

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:32.720
Is it actually the most
important meal of the day

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:34.280
like I was told my whole life?

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:35.641
- [Man] You can't be your best

00:01:35.641 --> 00:01:37.130
if you don't eat a good breakfast.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:39.340
- Turns out there's data
on this, lots of studies,

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:40.790
but you don't need to read them all.

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:41.960
In fact, don't read them all.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:43.730
Let me give you the 20-second version.

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:46.410
It turns out the methods
in a lot of these studies

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:47.530
are really crummy.

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:50.610
They weren't randomized controlled trials,

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:53.310
like the gold standard for good science,

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:54.890
and in a lot of cases,

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:56.940
they didn't really study anything useful.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.950
They kind of hyper focus on breakfast

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:00.390
as it pertains to weight.

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:01.700
And in most of these studies,

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.345
they don't even focus on what the person

00:02:03.345 --> 00:02:04.690
is eating for breakfast,

00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:07.860
they only focus on whether they
do or do not eat breakfast,

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:09.430
regardless of what
they're actually eating.

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:10.970
And to add to all of this,

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:12.910
a lot of these studies are funded

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:15.150
by corporations who have a vested interest

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:17.863
in making breakfast seem
necessary and healthy.

00:02:17.863 --> 00:02:19.910
So yeah, there's a lot of science on this,

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:23.120
but the science does not come
to any useful conclusions,

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.972
and is often times just poorly conducted.

00:02:25.972 --> 00:02:26.805
(screen snapping)
What this means

00:02:26.805 --> 00:02:28.360
is that there's no real evidence

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:30.570
that breakfast is the most
important meal of the day,

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:33.980
and there's nothing special
or magic about breakfast.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:36.960
That's more of a marketing
myth than anything else.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:38.750
- [Man] Had any really good days lately?

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:40.295
Like when the sun shines brighter,

00:02:40.295 --> 00:02:42.580
and people seem friendlier,

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:44.923
and work goes faster, things like that?

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:47.650
The really good days start out good

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:49.650
with new Carnation Instant Breakfast.

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:51.360
- Okay, wait, but there is
a little caveat in this.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.554
If you're an eight year
old kid or a teenager,

00:02:53.554 --> 00:02:55.400
and you're in school,

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:57.320
there actually is some useful evidence

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:59.074
that says that eating
breakfast in the morning

00:02:59.074 --> 00:03:02.880
is good for your learning
and brain development.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:04.330
So that's the caveat.

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:05.340
There actually is evidence there.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:06.540
For the rest of us though,

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:08.230
if you like breakfast in the morning,

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:09.430
eat breakfast in the morning.

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:11.930
If you're not into that, don't do it.

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:13.770
Okay, that's it.

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:14.603
Unless you're a kid,

00:03:14.603 --> 00:03:17.460
but I don't know how
many kids are watching.

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.260
Okay, so that's the science on this.

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:21.650
Turns out that I was a
person for many years

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:24.140
who thought that I didn't like breakfast,

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:26.320
that I didn't need to eat breakfast.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:28.030
Growing up in a small town in Oregon,

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:29.530
I didn't have a ton of exposure

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:30.910
to other cultures and other things,

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:33.771
and so my version of breakfast was this.

00:03:33.771 --> 00:03:36.240
- [Man] There's a bowl of Corn
flakes spooned from the dish.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:38.270
- [Man] And there was some bacon.

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:40.130
- This is all I knew as breakfast food.

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:43.060
When we would eat eggs
and bacon for dinner,

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:45.280
we were having breakfast for dinner.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.223
This food was inseparable from
the institution of breakfast.

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:51.620
If you're not from the United States,

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:54.100
let me just break down
what American breakfast is

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:56.190
and its story, where it came from.

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:58.290
I'll split it out into two main categories

00:03:58.290 --> 00:03:59.400
of breakfast in United States.

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.330
One, I called diner breakfast.

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:03.147
It kind of looks like this,

00:04:03.147 --> 00:04:03.980
(groovy music)

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:06.240
and the other, I will call cereal,

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:10.300
which is everything from
Pop Tarts to Froot Loops.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:11.760
- Where are the Froot Loops?

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:13.460
- I didn't bring any, help!

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:17.430
- To granola bars, to
Carnation Instant Breakfast.

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:19.700
It's like the quick stuff
that's in our pantries

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:21.590
that we can just consume before work.

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:24.330
First, let's talk about diner breakfast.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:27.300
There's no better reference
for what diner breakfast is

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:31.180
in the United States than
the official menu of IHOP,

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:33.570
which is so inappropriately named

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:36.623
as the International House of Pancakes.

00:04:36.623 --> 00:04:39.430
- Welcome to the International
House of Pancakes.

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:42.000
- At IHOP, this American institution,

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.860
you can start your day with this,

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:46.600
the Stuffed French Toast,

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:48.860
which effectively looks
like my birthday cake.

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:51.540
Or you can get really
cultured and hop over to Italy

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:53.250
for some Nutella crepes.

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:55.160
Or how about the Belgian Waffle Combo,

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:56.560
which isn't just a waffle,

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:59.320
but also comes with bacon and eggs?

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:00.820
Perhaps you're feeling savory,

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:02.620
so how about this breakfast sampler,

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:05.560
where you can eat a pig
in three different ways,

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:06.927
because who just wants to eat bacon

00:05:06.927 --> 00:05:08.350
when you could have all three?

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:10.700
All of this accompanied by French fries.

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:12.640
I mean, sorry, hash browns,

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:14.710
but why settle for these meager options

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:18.010
when you could go with
the Cinn-A-Stack Pancakes,

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:21.870
which come layered with a
luscious cinnamon roll filling

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:25.400
and drizzled with rich cream cheese icing,

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.020
and topped with, you
guessed it, whipped cream.

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:30.307
icing for breakfast.

00:05:30.307 --> 00:05:31.440
Now, here's the thing.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:36.440
This diner style American
breakfast is inspired by Europe.

00:05:36.827 --> 00:05:38.960
(bright music)

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:42.900
American pancakes look
a lot like other sweet,

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:46.440
flat unleavened breads that
are eaten all over the world

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:47.580
and especially in Europe,

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:50.550
we just deemed necessary
to fry it in butter

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:52.000
and then put more butter on top,

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:54.760
and then drizzle literal liquid sugar

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:56.930
over top and eat it for breakfast.

00:05:56.930 --> 00:05:58.580
That was the only modification we made

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:01.350
to this wonderful tradition
of European pancakes.

00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:05.229
The idea of eating pork-based
cured meats in the morning

00:06:05.229 --> 00:06:07.520
is also a European tradition.

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:10.120
It comes from the traditional
English breakfast,

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:11.590
which is delicious.

00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:14.243
We just decided to get rid
of some elements from that,

00:06:14.243 --> 00:06:15.270
like the beans,
(buzzer buzzing)

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:17.290
and the tomatoes, and the mushrooms.

00:06:17.290 --> 00:06:18.920
Who needs those, anyway?

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:19.870
- Where are the Froot Loops?

00:06:19.870 --> 00:06:23.010
- The American waffle
comes from the beautiful,

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:26.390
delicious tradition of the
Belgian or liege waffles,

00:06:26.390 --> 00:06:30.560
which are served in desert
stores as desserts in Belgium.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:31.470
They're delicious.

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:34.550
The modification we made was
to eat them for breakfast,

00:06:34.550 --> 00:06:36.840
and then decide to take out the best part,

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:38.110
which is the Pearl sugar,
(buzzer buzzing)

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:40.230
the crystallized sugar
that makes a Belgian waffle

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:42.300
or liege waffle delicious.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:46.870
To summarize, this diner style
took European traditions,

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:49.480
turned them into breakfast
and modified them

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:53.126
by making them lower
quality, bigger portions,

00:06:53.126 --> 00:06:58.126
and usually slathering them
with cream and maple syrup.

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:01.240
- [Man] All you can eat pancakes,

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:04.020
this calls for all you can eat silverware.

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:06.250
get IHOP's All You Can Eat Pancakes free

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:08.470
when you buy any breakfast combo.

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:10.606
- I mean, icing for breakfast?

00:07:10.606 --> 00:07:12.150
It just doesn't sound good.

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:14.330
Okay, so that's the diner
version of American breakfast,

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:17.730
a big, important category
of what Americans

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:19.250
like to eat for breakfast.

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:21.726
The other category looks like this.

00:07:21.726 --> 00:07:24.309
(groovy music)

00:07:28.450 --> 00:07:30.220
This is the stuff that's in our pantries.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:32.060
This is the stuff we eat
before we go to work.

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:34.405
It's quick, it's what I
grew up eating as a child,

00:07:34.405 --> 00:07:37.700
let's call this the cereal category.

00:07:37.700 --> 00:07:41.750
This food came into our
lives and became widespread

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:45.270
because of one main
force: industrialization.

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:47.580
- [Man] The factory whistle
awakened people in the mornings

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:50.150
and measured off their long days of toil.

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:54.640
- With the birth of factories
and the standardized workday,

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:57.646
suddenly you had demand for a consistent,

00:07:57.646 --> 00:08:00.540
quick, convenient food in the morning

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:01.800
to fuel you for the day.

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:03.960
Paired with that, you
also now had technology

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:07.680
to mass produce products
that would fit this demand.

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:11.720
Perhaps the best symbol of this
shift towards mass produced,

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:15.516
easy, convenient breakfast
food is Corn Flakes?

00:08:15.516 --> 00:08:16.930
- [Man] Kellogg's Corn Flakes.

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:19.190
- The Kellogg's corporation,
which made Corn Flakes

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:23.170
was a pioneer in making
this breakfast food popular.

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:25.620
Not only because they were
able to mass produce it

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:26.800
and cheaply sell it,

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:29.260
but also because they
marketed it in this way

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:31.010
that made it feel like
the most important thing

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:31.978
in the world.

00:08:31.978 --> 00:08:35.228
♪ Kellogg's adds extra vitamins ♪

00:08:35.228 --> 00:08:39.509
♪ To make their Corn Flakes
the best it's ever been! ♪

00:08:39.509 --> 00:08:40.500
(record scratching)
But there was a problem.

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:43.130
Over time, people would
look at this lousy bowl

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:45.390
of Corn Flakes and be like,
who wants to eat this?

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:46.740
This is boring.

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:48.730
But America had a solution.

00:08:48.730 --> 00:08:51.930
All we needed to do was
pump it full of sugar.

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:53.679
- [Man] One big gulp of it.

00:08:53.679 --> 00:08:54.630
- Yum!

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:55.880
- Gives me lots of energy--

00:08:55.880 --> 00:08:57.139
- [Man] for this nutritious breakfast.

00:08:57.139 --> 00:08:58.244
(icing bursting)
- [Man] This is how

00:08:58.244 --> 00:08:59.077
Pop Tarts have--
- Whoa!

00:08:59.077 --> 00:09:00.673
- Geez.

00:09:00.673 --> 00:09:03.650
I mean, that's, that's pretty
aggressive right there.

00:09:03.650 --> 00:09:04.483
Wow.

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:06.810
Yeah.

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:09.160
That's how I feel after
I eat a Pop Tart as well.

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:10.350
Anyway, people started to believe

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:13.370
that breakfast and
especially these commodities,

00:09:13.370 --> 00:09:16.423
which were quickly becoming
dessert disguised as breakfast.

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:18.955
- The ultimate fruit flavor.

00:09:18.955 --> 00:09:19.788
- Look!

00:09:19.788 --> 00:09:21.640
- Were the key to a healthy morning

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:22.880
and a good start to the day.

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:25.045
And soon, I was born into a world,

00:09:25.045 --> 00:09:28.430
a world where breakfast products and norms

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:30.160
were cemented before me.

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:31.785
A world where millions of Americans

00:09:31.785 --> 00:09:35.150
would wake up in the
morning and consume dessert

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:36.980
disguised as breakfast.

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:40.200
Your muffin is just a cupcake in disguise.

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:43.920
That cup of yogurt might as
well be a scoop of ice cream.

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:46.280
And then there's the mighty doughnut,

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:50.405
the food that doesn't even
pretend to be breakfast.

00:09:50.405 --> 00:09:52.110
It's just literal dessert,

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:53.560
and it's shamelessly just
like, hey, I'm dessert.

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:55.040
Do you want to eat me for breakfast?

00:09:55.040 --> 00:09:56.780
And a lot of people are like, yeah.

00:09:56.780 --> 00:09:57.870
I mean, I kind of respect that.

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:00.070
I'm kind of like, if
you're gonna be dessert,

00:10:00.070 --> 00:10:01.780
just represent yourself.

00:10:01.780 --> 00:10:03.180
I respect the donut.

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:04.740
But wait, let me make one thing clear.

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:07.877
My issues with this have
nothing to do with nutrition,

00:10:07.877 --> 00:10:10.460
even sugar or calories or any of that.

00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:13.430
I actually have a big
issue with our obsession

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:15.680
with categorizing food by its numbers

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:17.660
and its constituent parts.

00:10:17.660 --> 00:10:19.210
Not into that, I don't really care.

00:10:19.210 --> 00:10:22.630
My problem here is that
I don't like this food.

00:10:22.630 --> 00:10:25.641
I just genuinely do not want to wake up

00:10:25.641 --> 00:10:29.210
and consume dessert for breakfast.

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:32.470
If you do like this stuff for
breakfast, that's awesome.

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:33.890
I've no issues with you and I'm not going

00:10:33.890 --> 00:10:36.200
to try to convince you to change that.

